            <ArticleTitle>Phosphorylation-Dependent Feedback Inhibition of RIG-I by DAPK1 Identified by Kinome-wide siRNA Screening.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>403-415.e8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1097-2765(16)30864-4</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.molcel.2016.12.021</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Cell-autonomous induction of type I interferon must be stringently regulated. Rapid induction is key to control virus infection, whereas proper limitation of signaling is essential to prevent immunopathology and autoimmune disease. Using unbiased kinome-wide RNAi screening followed by thorough validation, we identified 22 factors that regulate RIG-I/IRF3 signaling activity. We describe a negative-feedback mechanism targeting RIG-I activity, which is mediated by death associated protein kinase 1 (DAPK1). RIG-I signaling triggers DAPK1 kinase activation, and active DAPK1 potently inhibits RIG-I stimulated IRF3 activity and interferon-beta production. DAPK1 phosphorylates RIG-I in vitro at previously reported as well as other sites that limit 5'ppp-dsRNA sensing and virtually abrogate RIG-I activation.</AbstractText>
